  <h2>What it does</h2>

  <p>
    Before the amendment, the church outside the United States was organised into
    <strong>central conferences</strong>, which could adapt the Book of Discipline
    to their own circumstances. The United States had no equivalent body. Its
    jurisdictional conferences elect bishops but cannot adapt the Discipline, so
    General Conference legislated for the United States directly — and, because
    General Conference legislates for everyone, United States practice became the
    default from which everywhere else adapted.
  </p>

  <p>
    The amendment replaces that arrangement with a single category. The central
    conferences and the United States alike become <strong>regional conferences</strong>,
    each with the same authority to adapt. General Conference keeps what is
    genuinely connectional and, by a 60% vote, decides what regions may not touch.
  </p>

  <p>
    Outside the United States much of this is a change of name: the central
    conferences were renamed regional conferences and carry on. The substantive
    new thing is a United States Regional Conference, which does not yet exist.
  </p>

  <div class="notice">
    <p>
      <strong>Two sets of paragraph numbers are in circulation.</strong> The ballot
      papers the annual conferences voted on were drafted against an older numbering
      and run one behind the 2024 Book of Discipline: the ballot's ¶31 is the
      Discipline's ¶32. This site uses Discipline numbers throughout and shows the
      ballot number alongside on every page.
      <a href="/sources#numbering">Why the numbers differ.</a>
    </p>
  </div>

